{
  "requirement": "Saved configuration MUST remain distinct from resolved runtime configuration.\nSerializing and deserializing a valid configuration MUST preserve its semantic\nvalues, including explicit versus omitted settings. Optional fields MAY be\nomitted on serialization; defaults and environment-derived values MUST NOT be\nmaterialized into omitted fields. TOML comments and formatting need not\nround-trip.\n\nLoading configuration or running a configured feature MUST NOT rewrite the user\nor project configuration as a side effect. Resolved credentials and live runtime\nobjects MUST NOT enter the serialized data structures. Explicitly supplied\ncredential values are permitted only in the fields documented by the authoring\nfeature; credentials resolved from a profile or environment MUST NOT be written\nback to configuration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no gap. Every obligation maps to marked code and to a test that runs it. The one soft spot is a possible marking gap on two unmarked helpers, covered under the second obligation.\n\n**Obligations, code and tests**\n\n1. **Saved configuration stays distinct from resolved runtime configuration.**\n   - The saved types are `UserConfig`, `WorkspaceConfig`, `WorkspaceAuthoringConfig`, `UserAuthoringConfig`, `AuthoringConfig`, `BedrockConfig` and `OllamaConfig` (`src/config.rs`).\n   - `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` (`src/config.rs` lines 280-292) is a separate type that derives only `Clone` and `Debug`. It is never serialized.\n   - `resolve_authoring_from` and `resolve_authoring_with_workspace_from` build the resolved value from the saved one.\n   - Tests: `workspace_defaults_and_configs_combine_with_existing_user_selections` and `authoring_concurrency_defaults_overrides_and_invalid_values`. The latter asserts `resolved.concurrency` is `None` while `effective_concurrency()` is derived, and that the saved TOML omits it.\n\n2. **Round-trip preserves semantic values, including explicit versus omitted settings.**\n   - The structs use `skip_serializing_if` on `Option`s and empty maps, and `UserAuthoringConfig.configs` has no skip so a present empty table is kept.\n   - `to_toml` and `parse` are marked.\n   - Tests: `backend_configuration_round_trips_without_materializing_defaults`, `user_and_workspace_authoring_tables_round_trip_every_map`, `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only` and `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it`. The `configs = {}` case is covered in `user_and_workspace_authoring_tables_round_trip_every_map`.\n   - In the workspace file an explicit empty `configs` serializes as omitted. That is semantically identical, because the field defaults to empty and nothing distinguishes the two cases.\n   - `OllamaConfig::endpoint`, `api_key` and `api_key_from` carry `{impl}` references to this requirement.\n   - `ResolvedAuthoringConfig::effective_concurrency` and `effective_policy` also resolve runtime defaults without writing them back, but carry no reference to this requirement. They implement `authoring.concurrency` and `authoring.policy`. I did not count the missing reference as a gap, but a strict reviewer could.\n\n3. **Defaults and environment-derived values are not materialized into omitted fields.**\n   - The Ollama endpoint default is applied only at read time in `OllamaConfig::endpoint()`. The environment key stays a name in `api_key_env`.\n   - The Bedrock profile and region stay `Option`s.\n   - Tests assert the output contains no `endpoint`, no `http://localhost` and no `concurrency` (`backend_configuration_round_trips_without_materializing_defaults`, `authoring_concurrency_defaults_overrides_and_invalid_values`).\n   - `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values` asserts the serialized form contains `MODEL_KEY` but not `private-value`.\n\n4. **Loading or running a configured feature does not rewrite the user or project configuration.**\n   - No configuration-writing code exists in `src/config.rs`. My search of `src` found `fs::write` only for evidence, usage, setup and the GitHub cache, and none of those target user or project configuration files.\n   - The loaders `load_user_config`, `load_workspace_config` and `UserConfig::parse` only read.\n   - Tests:\n     - `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only` asserts a missing file is not created and an existing file is unchanged.\n     - `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it` asserts the workspace file text is unchanged after loading.\n     - `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` asserts the user configuration bytes are unchanged after a run.\n     - `authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file` asserts both workspace files are unchanged and that no user configuration is created.\n\n5. **Resolved credentials and live runtime objects stay out of the serialized structures.**\n   - The only secret-bearing serializable types are `SecretString`, `BedrockCredentials` and `OllamaConfig.api_key`, which hold explicitly supplied values.\n   - `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` is not `Serialize`, and no runtime client is stored in any `Serialize` type.\n   - Tests: `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values`, plus the CLI test above, which resolves the key from the environment and checks the file is unchanged.\n\n6. **Explicit credential values appear only in the documented fields.**\n   - `deny_unknown_fields` on `BedrockConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `BedrockCredentials` limits the allowed fields, and `SecretString` is `transparent`.\n   - Test: `explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them` round-trips `credentials` and `api_key`, and rejects malformed or misplaced credential fields.\n\n7. **Credentials resolved from a profile or environment are not written back.**\n   - There is no write path. `api_key_from` returns a plain `String` and never stores it back into the struct.\n   - Tests: the Ollama environment test and the CLI unchanged-bytes assertions.\n\n**Marking.** The reference sites I read carry `{impl}` or `{test}` references to this requirement. Both of the following were checked against the requirement's text and found consistent with it:\n- The configuration code that carries out an obligation has an `{impl}` reference.\n- The tests that assert an obligation's outcome have `{test}` references.\n\n**Unspecified behavior.** None found. **FIXME references.** None.",
  "references": "6883306030f8222c8a254d9689d5c981595832114adc9c8d9427b25d95110c97",
